UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 33, Lesson 2: Triangles and quadrilaterals', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Triangles and quadrilaterals form families: triangles by sides/angles; quadrilaterals include squares, rectangles, rhombuses, trapeziums.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIs every square a rectangle? Is every rectangle a square? Explain.\n\nFEEDBACK\nYes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nClassify fourteen triangles and twelve quadrilaterals. Justify six classifications with properties.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Triangles and quadrilaterals form families: triangles by sides/angles; quadrilaterals include squares, rectangles, rhombuses, trapeziums.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Strategies","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-2DS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Classify fourteen triangles and twelve quadrilaterals. Justify six classifications with properties. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Yes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square. Reasoning: I used triangle by attending to quadrilateral rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties. Correction: Yes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square. Why: the correction restores accurate use of triangle.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses triangle with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with triangle, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Yes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square. This matches the lesson skill for triangle.","options":["Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Yes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square.","Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties."],"question":"Is every square a rectangle? Is every rectangle a square? Explain. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Triangles and quadrilaterals form families: triangles by sides/angles; quadrilaterals include squares, rectangles, rhombuses, trapeziums.","Yes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square.","Careful use of quadrilateral with clear evidence.","Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising triangle?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named quadrilateral, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the quadrilateral used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value."],"question":"A learner is practising triangle. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties.","Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about triangle?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Yes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Triangles and quadrilaterals form families: triangles by sides/angles; quadrilaterals include squares,… — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_