explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with triangle, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties. Correction: Yes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square. Why: the correction restores accurate use of triangle.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w33-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise triangle.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use triangle with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify quadrilateral and explain why it matters.","I can practise classify independently on a new prompt.","I can notice side needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Triangles and quadrilaterals form families: triangles by sides/angles; quadrilaterals include squares, rectangles, rhombuses, trapeziums. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Triangles and quadrilaterals form families: triangles by sides/angles; quadrilaterals include squares, rectangles, rhombuses, trapeziums.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about triangle?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEquilateral triangle: 3 equal sides. Isosceles: 2 equal. Scalene: no equal sides. Rectangle vs square: square is a special rectangle with equal sides.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIs every square a rectangle? Is every rectangle a square? Explain.\n\nFEEDBACK\nYes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square.","explain":"Triangles and quadrilaterals form families: triangles by sides/angles; quadrilaterals include squares, rectangles, rhombuses, trapeziums.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Triangles and quadrilaterals.","caption":"Use precise family language.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Triangle types</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">sides</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Quad family</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">square ⊂ rectangle</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Equilateral triangle: 3 equal sides. Isosceles: 2 equal. Scalene: no equal sides. Rectangle vs square: square is a speci Then write your own working.