orking with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Family inclusion vs appearance.","caption":"Justify six.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Square→rectangle</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">yes; reverse no</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tilt misclassify</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about triangle?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties. Here is a more productive model: Equilateral triangle: 3 equal sides. Isosceles: 2 equal. Scalene: no equal sides. Rectangle vs square: square is a special rectangle with equal sides.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nYes every square is a rectangle; no not every rectangle is a square.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Thinking a long thin diamond cannot be a rhombus if tilted ignores side properties. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no working.","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about triangle?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTriangles and quadrilaterals form families: triangles by sides/angles; quadrilaterals include squares, rectangles, rhombuses, trapeziums. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 33, Lesson 2: Triangles and quadrilaterals","unit_number":9,"unit_title":"Angles, shapes and transformations","week":33,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nEquilateral triangle: 3 equal sides. Isosceles: 2 equal. Scalene: no equal sides. Rectangle vs square: square is a special rectangle with equal sides.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Triangles and quadrilaterals.","caption":"Use precise family language.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><